# Copy to .env.local and fill in. The app and the CLI scripts read .env.local
# first, then .env — same precedence Next.js uses.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared SEO reference files  (required for reports)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Points at the unzipped Cowork skills folder — the one containing client-report/.
# The app reads client-report/references/current-state.md at runtime for the
# dated SEO facts and the confirmed Google update timeline.
#
# It will not fall back to facts baked into the source: with this unset, report
# generation refuses rather than writing a client deliverable from whatever was
# true whenever the code was written.
# SEO_REFERENCES_DIR=D:\CLAUDE\SKILLS\HS-Marketing Stack

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset -> embedded Postgres in ./.pglite (no account needed, real Postgres SQL).
# Set   -> any Postgres. Neon, Supabase, or your own.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Search Console
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Create a service account, enable the Search Console API, then add the service
# account's email as a user on each property in Search Console (read access is
# enough). Without these, the app runs on stored data and the sync button
# reports that it isn't configured.
# GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=hs-seo-ops@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# GOOGLE_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMII...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Team sign-in (Google OAuth)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From a Google Cloud project: create an OAuth client (Web application),
# add GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI as an authorised redirect URI, and enable:
#   - Search Console API
#   - Google Analytics Data API      (the Analytics screen)
#   - Google Analytics Admin API     (listing GA4 properties on /account)
# On the consent screen add both scopes:
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/webmasters.readonly
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/analytics.readonly
#
# Adding a scope invalidates existing sign-ins: refresh tokens carry the
# scopes they were issued with, so anyone who signed in before Analytics
# support must sign out and back in before GA4 will work for them.
#
# The first person to sign in becomes admin; everyone after that must already
# exist as a `users` row (an admin adds them) — sign-in is not open sign-up.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=xxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=xxxx
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback

# Signs the session cookie. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required before the cron endpoints will run at all — they refuse without one.
# Call them as: Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET
#   GET /api/cron/nightly                    -- pull Search Console data
#   GET /api/cron/weekly-reports             -- draft reports (run after the pull)
#   GET /api/cron/weekly-reports?cadence=monthly
# CRON_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately absent: there is no LLM API key.
# Reports are produced by a deterministic rule engine. See docs/hs-seo-ops-plan.md §4.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PageSpeed Insights (Core Web Vitals in site audits)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional but effectively required: without a key the request uses a shared
# anonymous quota that is routinely already exhausted, and the audit reports
# Core Web Vitals as "not checked" rather than guessing. Free key from
# https://developers.google.com/speed/docs/insights/v5/get-started
# PAGESPEED_API_KEY=
